WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.500
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Andrew Lakoff, who's a sociologist at the

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:10.890
University of Southern California, also author
of the 2017 book Unprepared Global Health

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.290
in a Time of Emergency.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:14.520
Great to have you, Andrew.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:15.870
Thanks for having me.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:22.530
So it's become very in vogue now to point
to, you know, Bill Gates and his TED talk

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:27.000
about at some point we're going to have a
pandemic that we're unprepared for.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.760
And so on and so forth.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:33.059
And I think for our conversation, I want to
focus in a little more on the nitty gritty

00:00:33.059 --> 00:00:37.239
that you've researched and reported on in
some of the specifics.

00:00:37.239 --> 00:00:40.879
Maybe to start with, we can talk about this
national stockpile.

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:43.690
What are the origins of the national stockpile?

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.260
What types of components are there?

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:51.379
We know, of course, about masks and ventilators
now, but what's the kind of the genesis of

00:00:51.379 --> 00:00:52.449
that stockpile?

00:00:52.449 --> 00:00:56.139
Well, there are two parts to the story.

00:00:56.139 --> 00:01:02.809
The very first idea of a stockpile of essential
supplies to be used in the event of a catastrophic

00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:05.630
event took place during the Cold War.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:11.610
So the Civil Defense Agency built up stockpiles
all around the country of essential medical

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:12.610
supplies.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:16.530
Now, by the 1970s, those, of course, hadn't
been used.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:20.100
Fortunately, we never had a nuclear war.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.630
That stockpile was closed down.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:28.219
But the idea was rekindled in the 1990s in
response to the threat of a bioterrorism attack.

00:01:28.219 --> 00:01:32.990
Officials in the Clinton administration were
worried that stockpiles of bio agents like

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:39.299
smallpox and anthrax that had been accumulated
in the Soviet Union had gone missing and might

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:40.880
be in the hands of terrorists.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:46.580
And they began to think about the need for
stockpiles of biomedical countermeasures in

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:47.909
the event of such an attack.

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:52.670
So that really it got put in place in the
late 1990s and early 2000s.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:57.810
And what what are examples of specifics that
are part of these stockpiles?

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:03.380
So initially, in response to the threat of
a bioterrorism attack, a lot of the elements

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:08.750
of the stockpile were specifically focused
on possible terror agents.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.380
So there were nerve gas antidotes.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:15.510
There was millions of vials of smallpox vaccine,
anthrax vaccines.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:21.130
So a lot of medications in cold storage, eventually
anti-viral medications in preparation for

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:22.400
pandemic influenza.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:28.720
There also were non-Mitt non-pharmaceutical
supplies, for example, and there have been

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:34.220
a lot of discussion of this ventilators, protective
equipment, that kind of thing when it comes

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.920
to the ventilator.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:41.330
I don't even really want to call it a debate,
but what was for a while fear of a shortage

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:46.480
and what I would characterize as the federal
government's slow implementation of distributing.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.690
I mean, I think that some of those ventilators
could have been distributed far earlier at

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:51.690
very little cost.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:55.540
If they end up not being necessary, OK, you
send them back.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.330
What else sort of should flesh out our understanding
of the ventilator piece specifically?

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:04.670
I mean, in terms of certainly creating new
ones, we know that the manufacturing process

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:05.810
is not.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:07.510
You have them in 48 hours.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:11.850
We're kind of now building up a supply which
will be manufactured over the next three months.

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:15.210
But what what insight do you have into the
entire ventilator situation?

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:22.380
Yeah, the I guess the two step back, one step
back a little bit.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:26.320
The important thing to keep in mind also is
the process of distribution.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:31.620
So a lot of planning and thought went into,
you know, once you have supplies available,

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:32.930
how do you decide where they should go?

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:34.680
How do you get them to the right places?

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:40.070
How do you keep track of how many supplies
of a given item you have and where they are

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.960
among the various stockpiles in different
parts of the country?

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.320
So one piece of the story is, of course, do
you have enough of them?

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:47.510
And that's the question you asked.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:51.110
But another is, how do you get them to the
places where they're needed?

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:52.110
Yes.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:58.570
And when it comes to that, is it unrealistic
of me to think that in late January or early

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:04.620
February, there were likely the signals that
would have supported starting to deploy them

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.990
to urban areas in some way?

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:10.621
Or is it unrealistic to think that that would
have been an appropriate time to do it, to

00:04:10.621 --> 00:04:11.660
be proactive?

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:12.660
Yes.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:20.079
An important part of stockpile planning is
to put in place the stockpiles of items where

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:23.960
they're needed as soon as you get a signal
that there might be a disaster on the way.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.710
So that's right.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:29.180
As the World Health Organization was putting
every nation in the world on alert in late

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:34.660
January, as officials were beginning to counsel
the administration that there might be an

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.770
epidemic of a very dangerous disease on the
way.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:39.530
In particular, a respiratory disease.

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:44.150
Indeed, that would have been the time to start
figuring out whether we had enough ventilators

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.870
and whether they were in the right place.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:49.790
So one more thing on the stockpile thing,
and then I want to move on to to a few other

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:50.790
things.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:54.990
Jared Kushner had this very strange Orwellian
moment where he said the stockpile is.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.140
Our stockpile.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:59.390
It's not the state stockpile.

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:00.390
I didn't.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.430
I don't even know what to make of that in
the sense that while everybody I guess unless

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:07.830
you're in Guam or Puerto Rico, you're in one
of the states like we are the states.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:14.210
But is there anything about the stockpiles
origin that makes that statement less Orwellian?

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:17.230
Well, it's a very confused statement.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:21.600
It doesn't understand the whole premise of
how governmental preparedness has to work

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.850
in a federal system like the United States.

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:28.760
The whole presumption of a system that is
run by the federal government is that states

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:32.680
would make requests to the federal government
for the items when they need them and that

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:36.801
there would be a elaborate and sophisticated
coordination between the federal government

00:05:36.801 --> 00:05:37.801
and the states.

00:05:37.801 --> 00:05:40.010
So it's not a competition between the federal
government and the states.

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:43.420
It's not that it's belongs to the federal
government or us.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:49.130
Whatever Jared Kushner meant by us, but rather
the real effort is to figure out what the

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:52.860
states need and how can we get that material
to them yet.

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:57.120
Elaborate and sophisticated is not matching
up in my mind with a lot of a lot of what

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.120
we've seen.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:05:59.290
So let's.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:04.760
What are some other ways in which as a planet
we are sort of underprepared for this type

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:07.100
of hundred year pandemic that we're experiencing
now?

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:09.850
I mean, we know we don't have a vaccine, but
that's a process.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:13.400
You need to sequence the virus and then there's
a process to go through.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:17.490
Most optimistically, there'd be no emergency
use vaccines available in the fall for health

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:18.490
care workers.

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:20.520
And then we're looking at 20 21.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:24.900
We talked about the ventilator distribution
component, although fortunately the actual

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.190
shortage in the US has been has been minimal.

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:32.980
What are some other ways that we're fundamentally
underprepared for dealing with something like

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:33.980
this?

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:38.430
Well, I think what we've seen in the last
couple of months is that you could have pretty

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:41.020
elaborate and sophisticated preparedness plans
in place.

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:45.300
And in fact, under the Obama administration,
very detailed playbooks were put together

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:50.840
for each step of coordinated response to a
potential dangerous epidemic or pandemic.

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:51.840
Yes.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.370
But if you don't if you have those in place,
but you don't know you've got the plans or

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:58.910
you're not you're not aware of the plans,
you haven't practiced them or you even don't

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:00.660
take them seriously.

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:05.670
Then when the actual event happens, you're
not going to you're not going to respond adequately.

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:09.080
But are there some other bottlenecks that
are very specific in terms of.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:12.680
I mean, you know, international travel is
very different now than it was during the

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:13.810
1918 flu pandemic.

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:20.699
And the speed with which pathogens can be
moved around is a part of it.

00:07:20.699 --> 00:07:25.370
Which systems are simply not prepared to really
deal with this at the start?

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:26.370
Yeah.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:30.680
I mean, I think that at that it's really a
variegated story around the world.

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:35.330
I think you can you can look at countries
like South Korea or Germany and see that although

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:38.970
of course, there's not yet a vaccine and there's
not even an effective medication.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:43.800
There are non-pharmaceutical interventions,
especially combined with widespread diagnostic

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:48.729
testing, that can do a decent job of mitigating
the disease threat.

00:07:48.729 --> 00:07:51.130
Obviously, they can't cure it.

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:54.830
But so so in certain countries that really
have thought carefully about how to do this

00:07:54.830 --> 00:08:02.150
and have strong health systems in place where
it looks not so catastrophic in other parts

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:05.770
of the world where you don't have competent
government, government leadership, you don't

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:10.620
have a really strong health system, you have
a lot of economic inequalities.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:14.120
That's where we see the catastrophic event
unfolding.

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:20.080
One of the things I've been pointing out is
that the virus has exposed the underlying

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:24.729
fragility of a lot of the systems that sometimes
feel very, very solid.

00:08:24.729 --> 00:08:29.130
And, you know, we could find two or three
months ago, we could have found a number of

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:31.690
indicators of the American economy that weren't
great.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:32.690
Right.

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:35.029
I mean, huge inequality and wages have been
stagnant, etc.

00:08:35.029 --> 00:08:39.180
But by the traditional measures, the economy
was humming along quite well, high consumer

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:41.880
confidence, low unemployment, high stock market,
etc.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:47.220
The fragility that is exposed by a complete
hundred and eighty degree turnaround in just

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:49.959
a few weeks for me has been one of the most
stunning things.

00:08:49.959 --> 00:08:54.360
And I don't know that, you know, the system
we have doesn't reward being proactive.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:58.310
It's not that profitable to be proactive,
but that's been a huge thing for me.

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:00.320
Yeah, no, I completely agree.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:03.009
Not only is the health systems for fragile.

00:09:03.009 --> 00:09:05.009
The supply chain system is fragile.

00:09:05.009 --> 00:09:10.490
The security of what we call essential workers
is precarious.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:16.279
One thing that preparedness planners didn't
think a lot about was the how to deal with

00:09:16.279 --> 00:09:21.790
the economic after effects of these non-pharmaceutical
interventions like social distancing measures.

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:27.850
So I think countries that have a much more
resilient economic system in place have turned

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:32.509
out to be much better prepared than countries
like ours that really focused on efficiencies

00:09:32.509 --> 00:09:34.139
at the expense of resilience.

00:09:34.139 --> 00:09:35.440
That's interesting.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:41.100
We know from a medical perspective with certain
countries were far better prepared.

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:45.230
South Korea certainly comes to mind, but which
are the countries that economically were much

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:46.230
better prepared?

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:52.430
Well, I think I would say the countries in
which it was taken for granted that you would

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:54.399
you would actually provide stable income.

00:09:54.399 --> 00:09:58.389
So you look at some of the Western European
countries that have, instead of furloughing

00:09:58.389 --> 00:10:03.579
workers or laying off workers, have kept workers
pretty much on the job and employed through

00:10:03.579 --> 00:10:05.220
government payments.

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:10.569
I think you need a you need a an assumption
of a social welfare system to pass that kind

00:10:10.569 --> 00:10:15.439
of legislation in an emergency context, which
is something we, of course, don't have here.

00:10:15.439 --> 00:10:17.259
Are there other countries?

00:10:17.259 --> 00:10:20.860
What other countries are at the top of the
list in terms of dealing with the medical

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:21.860
part?

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:22.899
I mean, what's happened in South Korea is
incredible.

00:10:22.899 --> 00:10:24.750
They still don't have 300 deaths.

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:25.750
Deaths.

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:26.750
They don't.

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:30.449
They're under 300 deaths, which is just stunning
given that their first case was officially

00:10:30.449 --> 00:10:33.209
found within hours of the first U.S. case.

00:10:33.209 --> 00:10:36.880
Germany has done well, although they had a
far larger outbreak.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:40.449
And I think it was just a little more advanced
by the time they got around to it.

00:10:40.449 --> 00:10:46.350
There is a lot of debate about what's happening
in Sweden, certainly a higher death rate now,

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:51.350
but you're sort of taking a bet that maybe
the subsequent waves won't be as deadly because

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:52.550
you'll have higher immunity.

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:53.550
We just don't know.

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:55.990
My my view is that we just won't know that
for a while.

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:58.619
What are the other countries that medically
have done the best?

00:10:58.619 --> 00:10:59.619
Yeah.

00:10:59.619 --> 00:11:02.889
And one thing I would I agree with you about
the uncertainty about the outcome in a place

00:11:02.889 --> 00:11:03.889
like Sweden.

00:11:03.889 --> 00:11:09.639
But one thing I would say is that insofar
as a claim is being made that we're kind of

00:11:09.639 --> 00:11:14.220
accidentally following the Swedish model in
Sweden, they have made sure that the health

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:19.379
systems are strong and that people, you know,
will have available medical care.

00:11:19.379 --> 00:11:22.550
So it's one thing to let things go without
a plan in place.

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:26.899
It's another thing to to sort of carefully
keep things less shut down and elsewhere when

00:11:26.899 --> 00:11:30.079
you know that you're gonna be able to provide
serious medical care for those who do get

00:11:30.079 --> 00:11:31.079
sick.

00:11:31.079 --> 00:11:34.619
I think the other countries to look at very
carefully, although, of course, there are

00:11:34.619 --> 00:11:38.139
different situations in the United States
are East Asian countries.

00:11:38.139 --> 00:11:45.199
So Taiwan, Singapore, places that experienced
stars were very proactive early on and in

00:11:45.199 --> 00:11:50.970
January, putting in place testing systems,
contact tracing systems, isolation practices,

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:55.769
in some cases closing down schools and businesses
early and in other cases in other cases not.

00:11:55.769 --> 00:11:59.800
But it looks like those countries have, for
the most part, weathered this much better

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:01.509
than the United States has.

00:12:01.509 --> 00:12:07.929
One of the ideas that's floated about when
we look at some of these other countries is

00:12:07.929 --> 00:12:14.399
that we can't really do what some of those
countries do because they are far more authoritarian

00:12:14.399 --> 00:12:16.939
now with the case of South Korea.

00:12:16.939 --> 00:12:21.569
South Korea ranks higher than the United States
on the democracy index, only slightly so.

00:12:21.569 --> 00:12:22.739
But but they do.

00:12:22.739 --> 00:12:28.119
And certainly to say that South Korea handled
that because they're an authoritarian system

00:12:28.119 --> 00:12:33.449
of government seems completely false when
it comes to some other places.

00:12:33.449 --> 00:12:38.059
Is there anything to the idea that not all
of these measures would be tolerable in the

00:12:38.059 --> 00:12:39.800
United States politically?

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:43.990
Well, I think, you know, again, just to add
to your point, a place like Germany, there's

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:48.449
no way you could make an argument that contemporary
Germany is more authoritarian than the United

00:12:48.449 --> 00:12:49.449
States.

00:12:49.449 --> 00:12:56.009
But it really has to do with competency, trust
and expertise, proactive government intervention

00:12:56.009 --> 00:12:58.989
and trust by the public in that intervention.

00:12:58.989 --> 00:13:04.470
So it's not so much a question of authoritarian
versus democratic, but when you have a when

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:07.880
you have a liberal democratic country, what
kind of system do you need to have in place?

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:12.480
Which, of course, we had we had planned for
such a system here that wouldn't invade fundamental

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:13.480
rights.

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:16.360
The problem is we didn't implement that plan.

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:19.410
Last thing I want to touch on going forward.

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:21.910
There may be a second and third wave.

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:23.769
There may just be a second one.

00:13:23.769 --> 00:13:25.769
This first wave may never really diminish.

00:13:25.769 --> 00:13:30.470
And it may sort of go into the time where
we would potentially have a second wave.

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:34.679
We don't know the timeline for a wide, widely
available vaccine.

00:13:34.679 --> 00:13:39.850
We don't exactly know when any of the 70 or
80 possible treatments may turn up to work

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:41.209
very effectively.

00:13:41.209 --> 00:13:42.569
There's economic concerns.

00:13:42.569 --> 00:13:44.670
We still aren't testing enough.

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:50.300
What are you looking at and what would be
the lowest hanging fruit to do over the next

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:51.889
six months?

00:13:51.889 --> 00:13:57.239
Well, I think what we what we're obviously
going to have a period of trial and error

00:13:57.239 --> 00:14:01.290
over the next six months as we figure out
what works and what doesn't work, because

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:06.790
obviously we won't have a vaccine in that
period or very it's very unlikely we would.

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:11.670
I think what we need what I'm looking at is
just the extent to which we're not flying

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:13.329
blind as we go forward.

00:14:13.329 --> 00:14:18.249
How much are we actually tracking what's going
on and proactively intervening before things

00:14:18.249 --> 00:14:19.249
get bad again?

00:14:19.249 --> 00:14:22.809
Each time we try to lessen our social distancing
measures.

00:14:22.809 --> 00:14:25.290
So the more information we have, the better.

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:29.999
And the more that information ties into actual
rapid policy decisions, the better outcome

00:14:29.999 --> 00:14:31.879
we'll have or over the next few months.

00:14:31.879 --> 00:14:36.220
So it sounds like at an absolute minimum,
no matter what the trajectory is going to

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:40.759
be, if we don't have dramatically more testing,
we are going to be short on the information

00:14:40.759 --> 00:14:42.709
we need to really make informed decisions.

00:14:42.709 --> 00:14:44.379
I think that's right.

00:14:44.379 --> 00:14:45.379
We need more.

00:14:45.379 --> 00:14:48.390
We need a dramatically more testing, but we
also need to new to, you know, what we're

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:50.230
gonna do with the results of those tests.

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:54.319
We actually need a plan that links tests to
policies.

00:14:54.319 --> 00:15:01.429
So it's not just the testing, but also acceptance
of guidelines from from a central coordinated

00:15:01.429 --> 00:15:07.089
government effort that will lead to less bad
outcomes.

00:15:07.089 --> 00:15:11.970
We've been speaking with Andrew Lakoff, sociologist
at the University of Southern California,

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:16.589
also author of the book Unprepared Global
Health in a Time of Emergency.

00:15:16.589 --> 00:15:18.649
Andrew, I really appreciate your time today.

00:15:18.649 --> 00:15:20.230
Thanks for having me here.

